patchFile('siman_015/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt', 'beer-hagolah', {
  '1#_': `Beraisa Chullin daf 65 and Beitzah daf 6; and like the first Tanna—Rif and Rambam chapter 3 of Laws of Forbidden Foods and Rashba in name of Seder Torah.`,
  '2#_': `Beraisa Shabbat daf 136; and like Rashbag.`,
  '3#_': `Statement of Rav Nachman, Chullin daf 58.`,
});

// --- beur-hagra (4) ---
patchFile('siman_015/beur-hagra/part-001.txt', 'beur-hagra', {
  '1#_': `<b>(Extract) A chick, etc.</b> In Chullin 64, as taught "all the creeping thing," etc.—words of R' Eliezer ben Yaakov (in Behemah HaMekishah); and therefore the rabbis disagree with him only where it went out into the air of the world, but if it did not go out into the air of the world—not so; and so in all books of poskim (and even for our girsa nevertheless this is clear in first chapter of Yoma daf 1—that not like Beit Yosef end of siman 86 who needed to infer from words of Rif and Rashba). And after, etc.—like the rabbis; and even though they said mishnat R' Elazar ben Azarya kav v'naki—specifically in the mishnah, but not in the beraisa. Terumat HaDeshen 54 ot 1; but the Rosh in first chapter of Yoma ruled like R' Eliezer ben Yaakov and wrote that even in the beraisa we rule like R' Eliezer ben Yaakov, as written in Yevamos (37) seif 1; and further wrote in Terumat HaDeshen and the Ran that Rav and Shmuel hold like them—the rabbis—from their dispute in first chapter of Yoma there on Yom Tov we learn that on a weekday R' Eliezer permits; and the stam regarding eyes not opened—from that R' Eliezer ben Yaakov said even on a weekday, etc.—according to his words; but Tosafot there s.v. "a chick" wrote it happened to coincide, etc. (end).`,
  '2#א': `<b>(Seif 2) That is, etc.</b> Bechoros 5a and Niddah 24a: there is in fetuses, etc.; and Tosefta chapter 3 of Chullin; and all chapters of Behemah HaMekishah—in a large animal it is.`,
  '2#ב': `<b>Until the beginning, etc.</b> Shabbat there.`,
  '3#_': `<b>(Seif 3) We do not rely, etc.</b> For a gentile is not believed except in woman's testimony mesisach l'fi tumo; and so in rabbinic matters; but in Torah law—not; and nevertheless siman 513 seif 6 in hagahah; and see siman 67 seif 11; and all the more in a matter where it is to enhance his merchandise—even to be stringent; and what is written "under his hand" is not believed, as written there.`,
});

// --- kereti (8) ---
patchFile('siman_015/kereti/part-001.txt', 'kereti', {
  '1#א': `So long as it has not gone out into the air of the world—and there is concern of sheretz creeping within the shell; and it is forbidden to break the shell to remove the chick, for it is like stillborn; and see below siman 66.`,
  '1#ב': `Permitted immediately—even if its eyes have not opened; and many are stringent that specifically eyes opened; and Bach is stringent until large feathers grow that birds are accustomed to; and see below siman 66.`,
  '2#א': `Its months completed—that is, they guarded it and no male came upon it from the beginning of nine months in a large animal and tested five months in a small one.`,
  '2#ב': `And five for a small one; and every month thirty days; and it does not give birth to premature ones; and even though they wrote in Even HaEzer that now nature changes regarding animal birth—we have nothing to innovate now except as Chazal said, and it is in the Gemara of Bechoros 20—see there. And see Rashi there who wrote days of animal birth are 271 days; and from this we learn a difficulty in a woman there are three days of absorption—and if so a woman conceives for 271 days; and in an animal Rashi decided to permit on 271 days, for there are no three days of absorption regarding an animal at all, and 271 days from when the male came upon it suffices—and then its months are called completed.`,
  '2#ג': `We are not concerned lest crushed—even if it had difficulty in birth, and also cannot stand, and all the more cannot walk—nevertheless we are not concerned, for we do not presume deficiency—the Rosh and Ran.`,
  '2#ד': `Its months completed—see Peleti that I wrote according to Maharshal and rabbis of Speyer in Rokeach their view that in such a case one must spread on the ground.`,
  '2#ה': `Its months—see Peleti that I also copied Rama's words in Darkei Moshe and Shulchan Aruch Orach Chaim that all this is for all days of the year, but on Yom Tov it is forbidden to slaughter lest treifah be found after shechitah and one slaughter on Yom Tov in vain; and see Peleti that I clarified his words at length from sugyot of the Gemara; and also even on Yom Tov if there is no deficiency that it cannot spread—it is permitted and one need not see with eyes that it spreads on the ground—see there.`,
  '3#_': `We do not rely—Taz wrote it is not a double doubt, doubt completed or not completed, and if you say not completed perhaps it waited, etc.; and there was an error in his writing—for certainly there is no double doubt here: if one can say "if you find to say not completed," you still cannot say "perhaps it waited"—what of it if not completed, behold it is full stillborn and no waiting helps at all. Only the reverse doubt: doubt whether it waited eight days, and if you say it did not wait, perhaps its months completed—and on this Taz explained it is all in the category of one name of coercion, whether stillborn or not. And he wrote well; and in vain Shach attacked him in Nekudat HaKesef. In kids and lambs we are not expert in completed months and therefore always need waiting. And later authorities gave a sign in teeth and horns.`,
});

// --- peleti (3) ---
patchFile('siman_015/peleti/part-001.txt', 'peleti', {
  '1#_': `Lest its limbs were crushed from birth pains—and this is as the Ran wrote: even if it struggled at birth, nevertheless we are not concerned, for we follow majority—and majority of offspring even in great difficulty nevertheless offspring are healthy and strong—so from Ran's words. And it must be said this is not when the animal struggled entirely with deficiency—for otherwise why would the Gemara bring support for Rav Nachman from a calf born on Yom Tov, lest there the mother did not struggle. And it must be said the Gemara holds as simple: without difficulty we are not concerned for crushing, and even with difficulty we are not concerned. And so Tosafot and Rosh raised—even if it cannot stand (and in Tosafot it says cannot walk, but the essence is like Rosh: cannot stand). And this is when we see clearly it cannot stand—for otherwise why need Tosafot and Rosh for proof, behold the Gemara is arranged regarding spreading on ground that all agree it is fit and Rav Nachman's words are unnecessary. Only there we see it stood and it is simple there is no concern only if we do not see whether it stood or not—then without Rav Nachman in any case checking is required, and Tosafot and Rosh teach that even this Rav Nachman does not teach—only he teaches regarding deficiency before us that we see it cannot stand or walk, nevertheless we are not concerned; and see Tosafot and Maharsha—and it is simple.`,
  '2#_': `And if it is not known, etc.—the Rosh in first chapter of Beitzah wrote: "and that which he permitted to eat it on that day—that is when it is known its months are completed; but if it is not established to him that its months are completed, it is forbidden until the eighth night, as stated in chapter R' Eliezer of Milah; and also it must spread on the ground to remove doubt of crushing limbs, as stated in first chapter"—end of his words. And therefore the Tur in Orach Chaim and all challenged him—for this is against halachah that we rule the womb has no concern of crushing limbs; and the Gemara sought to support Rav Nachman from that calf born and we see it spread on the ground. But according to what we rule like Rav Nachman, there is nothing of R' Eliezer even if it did not spread at all—and as I wrote in name of the Rosh himself: even if it did not stand, and many commentators reconciled him. And I grasped first the view of Maharshal in Yeshuot Yaakov Chullin who explained the Rosh's intent: certainly when established its months completed, spreading on ground is unnecessary—as the Rosh wrote in first chapter; only because it is not established internally, eight-day waiting is required and also spreading on ground, and support from rabbis of Speyer brought in Rokeach. And Bach and Taz wondered on this—for he wrote as explained in first chapter and there is no mention at all of "its months completed" requiring spreading on ground; and also what relation have completed months to spreading on ground. And it appears to reconcile without rejecting the pleasant ones with both hands: and let us repeat why we require spreading—it must be said since months not completed it is very weak and there is more concern for crushing limbs—this is complete error: if truly not nine full months, behold full stillborn and waiting eight days and more does not help; only that eight days' waiting is testimony it is complete offspring and months finished. And since so, the question stands: what distinguishes this offspring from offspring we know is nine months, that it depends on knowledge. And if we say eight days alone do not testify months completed except combined with spreading hoof on ground—then all the reason in our sugya that spreading removes stillborn and shows months completed; however the Rosh did not say this, only to clarify there is no crushing—and if we say certainly if it does not walk on day born there is no deficiency of birth weakness, but this one waited eight days and nevertheless does not walk we are concerned for crushing. For if so in all offspring even when we know months completed and they do not walk several days after birth we would concern for crushing. Therefore it appears to say he holds that eight days' waiting for completed months is not in all offspring—there are offspring whose months did not fill and nevertheless waited many days as naturalists testified; only majority of offspring are not so; and in all Torah we follow majority—and majority waiting eight days are excellent offspring. And that the womb is not concerned for crushing is because majority of offspring even if cannot stand nevertheless have no crushing; but combine two minorities: minority not completed months and minority cannot stand—the majority is impaired, behold doubt and forbidden; and therefore we require spreading on ground and there is no concern at all—and this is correct in my humble opinion, and on this rabbis of Speyer in Rokeach intended. However still the Rosh's answer is needed—he wrote and it implies first chapter and there is no mention and no proof at all; and it appears what I already wrote in previous siman what is difficult in the sugya there that challenges from beraisa calf born on Yom Tov and answers yotzei dofen and again challenges from "and equal" if born with its blemish with it that this is prepared and answers it spread on ground—and does not answer immediately on the first beraisa thus; and also why call it "two" regarding spreading or standing. And it appears in the beraisa that Rav taught: calf born on Yom Tov permitted, chick forbidden—for this is prepared through its mother and this is not prepared. And see Maharsha who raised for Rav's ruling that it permits itself through shechitah if calf born eight days before Yom Tov and it is not established internally that months completed—forbidden on Yom Tov as muktzeh and prepared through mother does not apply—see there. And I expanded on this root in my work on Rambam's laws of Yom Tov. And therefore one may challenge: why teach calf and chick—teach all calf; and in such case calf whose months completed born on Yom Tov permitted as prepared through mother, but whose months not completed born before Yom Tov and on Yom Tov completed eight days—forbidden to slaughter as not prepared through mother; and chick would be learned a fortiori forbidden as prepared through mother does not apply at all. And one may say the beraisa incidentally taught us that even without spreading on ground born on Yom Tov permitted in shechitah and we are not concerned for crushing—and this is learned since it teaches stam born on Yom Tov and does not teach "it stood," and stam offspring does not stand on day born; and similar to end chick born on Yom Tov where standing does not apply at all. And this if it teaches chick; but if it teaches born and not established completed, etc., as above—certainly it deals with spreading, for without this forbidden as Maharshal above wrote; and perhaps the first part deals where it spread on ground. And we did not know that without standing also we are not concerned for crushing limbs—and this is the Gemara's difficulty challenging from calf born on Yom Tov intending that beraisa of Rav taught there, and proved as I wrote it does not deal with spreading as above, and brings support well for Rav. And the Gemara answers yotzei dofen—and if so no longer difficult—let it teach in the end where we do not know months completed and on Yom Tov completed eight days as above—one may say that which we rely on eight days' waiting to say certainly not stillborn is because otherwise majority supports—majority animals excellent offspring and she does not miscarry twins—and this eight days' waiting causes permission; and see Tosafot Shabbat and Bechoros in several places. And this in birth where majority animals do not miscarry stillborns; but if the womb was cut and the fetus extracted, the majority is that truly the animal did not give birth and for what was alone we do not rely. And if so, "not completed months" is unnecessary and all reconciled—and requires study. And Maharshal's words are required. But with all this Moses is true and his Torah true—Rama in Darkei Moshe, for from his place it is decided the Rosh wrote in Beitzah and not Chullin; only the Rosh admits all year permitted, but on Yom Tov not, lest treifah be found in inner organs and slaughter on Yom Tov without need; and he sensed himself the Taz's and later authorities' difficulty that we rule crushed bird permitted to slaughter on Yom Tov, and we answer from "one may not whiten roof tiles." And Rama's words there: it has presumption—and this born needs no presumption—this is essence of his words; see similarly. And Magen Avraham already challenged: also there there is no clear presumption—it must be said clear from force of majority, and even there majority excellent offspring. And the essence difficulty: what proof from first chapter where there is no proof at all—and would that it were not to contradict. And it appears Tosafot raised there in Chullin that even Rav Nachman deals only where there is deficiency that cannot stand and walk; but where there is no deficiency, all agree we are not concerned for crushing, for we follow majority of offspring. And they cry in the house of study: if so what did the Gemara resolve from those two beraisot of born on Yom Tov—perhaps there it deals where there is no deficiency, and why need the Gemara to answer it spread—in short it should reject there is no deficiency. And it appears certainly according to Rama's words that even on a weekday there is no concern, for we are not concerned for crushing; on Yom Tov there is concern to slaughter it on Yom Tov. And if so when we judge an animal because of crushing organs we do not judge whether permitted or forbidden—only whether permitted to slaughter on Yom Tov or forbidden. And if so it is a davar sheyesh lo matirin, for he can wait until weekdays and slaughter without doubt; and I already wrote above siman 1 in name of Ran: in a davar sheyesh lo matirin we do not follow majority, for until you rely on majority wait until permission comes—and one need not certainly majority kosher animals without crushing—only until you slaughter on Yom Tov and rely on majority, wait until tomorrow and one need not majority for shechitah. And these are the Gemara's words supporting Rav Nachman that the womb has no concern of crushing at all; and therefore even if impaired and cannot walk and stand, nevertheless there is no deficiency—for there is concern only regarding standing we are not concerned, for we follow majority. If so on Yom Tov slaughter we do not follow majority as above, for it is davar sheyesh lo matirin—how slaughter on Yom Tov even if fit to stand. Or say there is no concern at all for crushing. And the Gemara's rejection: forever without ability to walk we are concerned; and regarding fit to walk, permitted from majority of offspring as Tosafot wrote—and this is truly slaughter on Yom Tov forbidden, for we do not follow majority—only it deals where certainly it spread before us, and not regarding fit to spread, and certainly it spread—then there is no concern and majority unnecessary and slaughter on Yom Tov permitted—and if you find to say. And if so even though we rule like Rav Nachman, nevertheless we learn from his words that if there is deficiency that cannot stand, we validate from majority; and if so on Yom Tov slaughter forbidden as above; but if proof to stand, according to Rav Nachman majority unnecessary and permitted on Yom Tov—and therefore it must be said what Rosh and Tur wrote that spreading does not mean clearly spread, only appearance of spreading to exclude cannot stand. However this does not seem in category of davar sheyesh lo matirin—for chametz on Pesach poskim hold it is not davar sheyesh lo matirin returning to prohibition next year, and one cannot say eat it in permission now. And if so also on first day of Pesach when seventh of Pesach arrives it will also be forbidden to slaughter for the aforementioned reason—and it returns to prohibition. But this is not so—for since it stood, there is no further concern; so too waiting from time to time—one rule applies to standing and waiting from time to time. And if so since born on first Yom Tov before second Yom Tov arrived, already passed several from time to time and no longer prohibition concern and davar sheyesh lo matirin applies—unlike fallen where standing does not help and waiting from time to time does not help until it walks or waits twelve hours, and still can return to prohibition if it did not walk and second Yom Tov arrived. And if so it is not called davar sheyesh lo matirin since possibility to return to prohibition; and since no davar sheyesh lo matirin we rely on majority and permitted to slaughter—and requires study. And with what I wrote, the Rosh's intent that spreading means there is no deficiency is reconciled—Peri Chadash in Orach Chaim challenged the Rosh: what support for Rav Nachman from calf born on Yom Tov—nevertheless spreading required to slaughter on Yom Tov; and if it deals without spreading there is no support; according to what I wrote it is fine that he knows there is no deficiency—only without Rav Nachman's words it would not help because of davar sheyesh lo matirin as above. However in any case I answered Peri Chadash's difficulty: one may infer in the Gemara that it spread and Rashi explained it extended its leg to stand, as they said "extended its hand to stand," etc.; and one must understand what Rashi sought from this until he brought proof on it and did not explain simply it truly spread. And it appears certainly it did not escape that it deals with standing—only this is not in reality in one moment when offspring is born it will stand on ground; and if so according to one who holds muktzeh for half Shabbat, this moment before standing would be muktzeh lest perhaps it will not stand and slaughter forbidden from crushing concern. And similarly they said regarding firstborn born with its blemish—at moment before they saw blemish it was set aside; and they answer court sat there on Yom Tov—and if so here standing is unnecessary—what does it help, and all the more there is no crushing concern at all (and this supports you—Ran holds on Yom Tov muktzeh at beginning of Beitzah; according to one who rules like R' Shimon none of this applies). And the Gemara answers it spread—Rashi: not actual spreading, which also is not in reality immediately at birth—only extended legs, and this can be true immediately at birth and there is no muktzeh for half Shabbat—and requires study. And if so even Peri Chadash's difficulty is reconciled—for truly even according to Rav Nachman spreading is only this, only l'chatchila to permit slaughter as above, and there is no muktzeh for half Shabbat concern—as we rule regarding tevel it is not called muktzeh since if one transgressed and fixed it, it is fixed—and requires study. And indeed if Tosafot's words on Rav Nachman's matter were without halachah and did not write like Rosh regarding without standing—one may say Tosafot truly hold if it stood checking is required, and so Gaon Maharig in his responsa brought in Chavot Yair. How do we say it spread, etc.—it is like standing, and we rule standing requires checking—and this should have been in Beitzah regarding crushed bird. And requires study—for we already challenged why not answer on that beraisa calf born on Yom Tov, etc. And according to the above it is fine that certainly it is not common it will walk immediately, only can stand; and if it stood, the Gaon's difficulty arises—resolve, etc.; but in second beraisa where in any case we establish there in Beitzah b'dieved and l'chatchila forbidden to slaughter, they answer well it spread and it is like standing—and if so how permitted l'chatchila to slaughter—for truly forbidden only if slaughtered there is no crushing concern. And with this Peri Chadash's difficulty is also reconciled—for without Rav Nachman's difficulty it is not hard according to Rosh's view that Rosh admits if it stood no checking needed since we are not concerned for crushing; and if so the Gemara knew it deals with standing and no difficulty—resolve that no checking needed at all—and requires study.`,
  '3#_': `We do not rely, etc.—Shach copied Rashba's responsa words that a gentile is not believed except in woman's testimony—from which even mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed here, for even regarding woman he is not believed except mesisach l'fi tumo. And he inferred well; however it is difficult on its body: behold this is only rabbinic prohibition—for from Torah even without waiting at all it is permitted, for we follow majority and majority of offspring are born complete months and excellent offspring; and therefore we rule from Torah no need for waiting; and see Tosafot Bechoros daf 20a "milk exempt" who gave reason why they were stringent rabbinically to require eight days and we do not follow majority. And apparently from Tosafot it is proved he does not hold like Ran that in davar sheyesh lo matirin we do not follow majority—for otherwise what difficulty why not follow majority excellent offspring—for he can wait eight days and it is permitted and it is davar sheyesh lo matirin. But in truth this is error—for Ran said where Chazal already fixed prohibition, like egg born on Yom Tov that is forbidden—and we come to judge this egg whether we follow majority it was not born on Yom Tov—on this we say it is davar sheyesh lo matirin; but here we judge that Chazal had no reason to fix prohibition at all except to rely on majority—in this Ran's law does not apply, and womb has no crushing concern; and Tosafot gave reason we follow majority. And even we may say wait until from time to time—only because of majority Chazal did not decree initially; and also the aforementioned regarding gentile—and requires study, for correct. And in my chiddushim I answered Tosafot's question—for we say in the Gemara that without following majority one could not eat meat lest perhaps at place of perforation he slaughtered—only we follow majority. And if so without waiting, behold one minority perhaps at perforation he slaughtered, and another minority perhaps it is stillborn—and combine two minorities and impaired; and similar reasoning Ran wrote regarding buying eggs lest impure bird—combine two minorities; and this too is similar—and requires study. In any case according to all it is only rabbinic prohibition; however from many things in Rashba it is evident his view that even in rabbinic prohibition a gentile is not believed mesisach l'fi tumo, and therefore he copied here; but according to what we rule in several places in Shulchan Aruch and Rama that in rabbinic matters mesisach l'fi tumo is believed—even here he is believed—and requires study.`,
});

// --- pitchei-teshuva (3) ---
patchFile('siman_015/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt', 'pitchei-teshuva', {
  '1#_': `<b>Lest they were crushed.</b> See Taz what he attacked on Maharshal; and see responsa Brit Avraham part Yoreh De'ah siman 11 that he vindicated Maharshal's words in good reason; and see responsa at end of his Shabbat siddur in middle of responsa 4.`,
  '2#_': `<b>Beginning of the eighth night.</b> See Pri Megadim who wrote we require seven days from time to time pass; and if born end of day 1, if beginning of Motzei Shabbat night arrived it still did not leave category of stillborn until end of next day—see there; and so implied from Taz's language s.k. 2 who uses language from time to time. However in responsa Noda B'Yehuda tinyana part Yoreh De'ah siman 7 in responsa of the author's son he expanded and brought many proofs that from time to time is not required; and wrote so is Taz's view in siman 14; and concluded nevertheless if a case comes to my hand it is hard for me to be lenient since it is explicit in famous decisors for prohibition; and nevertheless one who is lenient does not lose, since in any case it is only rabbinic prohibition (see on this in Dagul MeRevavah and responsa Noda B'Yehuda tinyana part Even HaEzer siman 19 and in his book responsa Shivat Tzion siman 24 if it is clear those seven days are only rabbinically)—see there. Again I saw in new Pri Megadim books printed in name of one great one who wrote on Pri Megadim's aforementioned words they are mistaken words [now I saw the rabbi z"l himself retracted in his book Pri Megadim Orach Chaim siman 498 in Mishbetzot s.k. 2 see there; also in his book Teivat Gome chapter Re'eh]. Also in last responsa Beit Shmuel part Yoreh De'ah siman 11 he raised from time to time is not required—see there; and so raised in responsa Teshuvah MeAhavah part 1 siman 196 and 197—see there; and so agreed in responsa Rabbeinu Akiva Eiger siman 257—see there [and in responsa of his son-in-law Gaon Chatam Sofer siman 17]. And b'dieved if they slaughtered within eight—see in Dagul MeRevavah what its law. And see in s.k. after it.`,
  '3#_': `<b>We do not rely.</b> See Bach s.k. 5 in name of Taz what he wrote and we do not say it is a double doubt, etc.; and see in book Nekudat HaKesef who wrote there is no beginning to this question how can you say "if you find to say not completed perhaps it has eight—what of it," etc.; and see in Sha'ar HaMelech laws of Yom Tov din 20 what he challenged Nekudat HaKesef on this; and see in responsa Bigdei Kehunah part 4 siman 1 who answered his difficulty—see there; and see further in responsa Noda B'Yehuda tinyana part 4 siman 6; and see in book Levushei Serad in chiddushim ot 2 that they asked regarding one city accustomed to slaughter calves purchased from gentiles and it is unknown if they are eight days old and they did so because of lack of knowledge; and afterward a sage came and informed them it is forbidden—what is the law of utensils cooked in them until now, and he ruled those utensils that were one-day old when the sage informed of the prohibition are forbidden forever, even if what was cooked in them was from a somewhat large calf that to appearance is eight days old; but utensils that were not then one-day old are permitted (except utensils requiring hag'alah—hag'ale them), even if what was cooked was from a small calf that to appearance was not eight days old—see there.`,
});

// --- rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd (3) ---
patchFile('siman_015/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt', 'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd', {
  '1#_': `(Siman 15, Taz s.k. 1) <b>And we return to the principle of individual and many, etc.</b> That which is decided for him in the Rif's view that this principle of mishnat R' Elazar ben Azarya kav v'naki applies even where many disagree with him—it appears to me that the Rif in chapter Ha'Ediyot HaDayanim who said sometimes a person swears on his own claim and the rabbis disagree with him—and the Rif wrote halachah is like R' Elazar ben Azarya from his mishnah kav v'naki—behold this principle was stated even where many disagree with him—see there; and in Ran who wrote the Geonim ruled there like the rabbis—he holds this principle does not exist where many disagree with him—see there.`,
  '2#_': `(There, Taz s.k. 2) <b>And if so why forbidden, etc., seven times from time to time.</b> From this language it implies seven times from time to time are required; and one may say part of the day of birth counts as all of it; and so Pri Megadim wrote; but Taz wrote simply to be lenient; and in my humble opinion the essence is like Taz—for Rashbag relied on verse "seven days it shall be with its mother" and there from time to time is not required, as proved in Tosafot Arachin (daf 18b) s.v. "from its mishnah." They proved we count years in korban from time of birth and not from time of designation. From that we say hours disqualify in kodshim—that if between shechitah and sprinkling it became a year old it is invalid. And if it were that we count from designation, you would not find this law—see there; behold it was simple to them that for designation from time to time is not required; and if so you again find the year completes from designation between shechitah and sprinkling—as explained in my humble opinion with God's help.`,
  '3#_': `(Taz s.k. 4) <b>It is difficult—for behold it is a double doubt.</b> In Nekudat HaKesef he answered: how can you say "if you find to say not completed perhaps it has eight"—nevertheless not completed and it is stillborn. And in Sha'ar HaMelech chapter 1 laws of Yom Tov he contradicted this—for Peri Chadash raised in Rashba's view that double doubt turning over is not required; and if so still there is double doubt: doubt whether it waited eight days, and if you say it did not wait, perhaps months completed—see there. And in my humble opinion it is not so—for according to Shach's view that certainly not completed, eight days do not help; and if so there is no double doubt here—all is one, for we say perhaps it waited eight days and it is proved months completed, and if you say it did not wait eight days, perhaps without proof months completed; and if so all doubt is only whether months completed—and simple. And see in Teshuvot HaTashbetz (part 4 ch. 1 siman 2) who wrote for truth of the matter to permit stam offspring through double doubt; and that of Rashba that we do not rely on gentile mesisach l'fi tumo deals with smaller ones that resemble more that they did not wait eight days—see there. Afterward a rav came to my hand the book Ba'er Heitev Yoreh De'ah from the Gaavad of Berlin z"l and I saw he brought these Teshuvot HaTashbetz words and concluded: nevertheless from it at least one may rely well on signs Knesset HaGedolah brought; and so what Peri Chadash brought in name of Maharikash—end of his words. <b>I was asked</b> by one sage, thus: two witnesses contradicting each other in their testimony on a calf slaughtered whether it was five days old or not—for it is impossible one witness is believed to testify it is eight days old to remove it from presumption of stillborn; and there is room to side that he is believed—for all in his hand is believed even in presumption of prohibition; and so here it was in his hand to wait eight days. And one may also say there is no presumption of prohibition since permission comes automatically—and similar to what Tosafot Gittin (daf 2b) s.v. "one witness" wrote—end of his words. <b>Answer:</b> Behold all his words here are without supervision—for if we suppose there is presumption of stillborn, it is not called "in his hand"—for if it is stillborn, eight days will not be fulfilled. Also it is not similar to Tosafot Gittin—for there the essence of Tosafot's reasoning is what she says now that she counted seven clean days is not against presumption—for what we knew before that she saw blood before seven days, that evidence does not damage and does not cause prohibition now after seven days passed; for because of that evidence alone she is permitted now since seven days passed—only she comes to forbid lest she also saw afterward; and on this she was not presumed to see again—therefore regarding the other evidence it is not called presumption of prohibition; unlike here where presumption of stillborn causes it to remain in prohibition forever—there is full presumption of prohibition. And truly in essence of law the refutation that there is no presumption of stillborn at all—for from moment formed there is doubt whether viable or not; and witness testifying it is eight days old is like witness testifying it was clarified it was never stillborn and was always permitted; if so witness did not come to remove from presumption, only to clarify there was never presumption of prohibition—and simple. <b>However</b> what requires study—one may say there is presumption of pregnant and now she gave birth and it is not eight days old; and see Bava Metzia (daf 100) in Tosafot s.v. "this is," Sumchus, etc.; and I expanded elsewhere in explanation of their words with God's help—that it implies there is presumption, only it is not so strong against presumption of majority; also one may say since if stillborn its shechitah does not purify it and it is as if dead of itself—if so after shechitah this offspring has presumption of prohibition that it is not slaughtered, like any doubt in shechitah; and even if permissive witness came before shechitah while offspring alive—even according to his words it is still not slaughtered and does not remove from presumption, and his testimony only that there is remedy in shechitah—and this is not against presumption that all animals have remedy in shechitah. Nevertheless in my humble opinion in what he testifies it will be permitted after shechitah—when we come to permit eating through this witness's testimony, we remove from presumption of prohibition "not slaughtered"—here too one witness is not believed. And proof from sugya beginning of Gittin that they challenged and required two, etc.—it is presumption of prohibition even though there the agent testifies before divorce that this get is fit to divorce with, written for her name—and with this now he does not remove her from presumption, for even according to his testimony now she is a married woman—only he testifies it is possible to divorce her with this get, and this is not against presumption, for it is simple he can divorce with fit get; and nevertheless it is called presumption of prohibition in what is judged after giving through this witness's testimony. And if so a fortiori there—at hour he testifies certainly in husband's hand to divorce her with fit get and remove from presumption, all the more in our case where it is not in his hand to remove presumption lest stillborn and eight days will not be fulfilled. And somewhat supported our case is called presumption of prohibition from what we rule gentile mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed to testify they are eight days—and the reason explained in Rashba's responsa (siman 243) that gentile mesisach l'fi tumo is believed only in woman's testimony alone; and it is hard apparently—for from Torah we follow majority, majority of offspring are viable, and rabbis required eight days as Tosafot Yevamos (daf 119) wrote; and from Torah we ruled in Even HaEzer (siman 256) regarding child dying within thirty, if kohen's wife she does not perform chalitzah—and therefore from Torah no need for stillborn, for we follow majority; and in all rabbinic matters gentile mesisach l'fi tumo is believed as terumah rabbinic as stated in Bava Kamma (daf 119); and one should not distinguish that in terumah the prohibition itself is rabbinic but here the root of stillborn prohibition is Torah—even though from Torah we follow majority, nevertheless since Chazal were stringent and concerned for stillborn it is like Torah and mesisach l'fi tumo does not help—so one may study in Magen Avraham (siman 513) who wrote in name of Maharshal in Beitzah eggs brought to sell on Yom Tov after Shabbat that gentile mesisach l'fi tumo is believed they were born before Yom Tov, since from Torah we follow majority—only rabbinically forbidden as davar sheyesh lo matirin, behold even though preparation there is Torah prohibition, nevertheless since from Torah we follow majority and concern is rabbinic, mesisach l'fi tumo is believed—if so here too. However in my humble opinion to be lenient: presumption of pregnant is not excellent presumption, since we cannot say "now she gave birth" and not limit time when she gave birth—it is not presumption according to Rashba in Tosefta Yevamos (siman 81) regarding cheeses; and even though we do not rule like Rashba, nevertheless according to Taz below (siman 497 s.k. 2) who wrote regarding dead before us the presumption of life was impaired even though in all presumptions we say "now it was impaired"—life presumption is different as it stands for that and will certainly die—see there—so too presumption of pregnant will certainly be removed, behold she gave birth before us. And I have proof for Taz from Tosafot Yevamos (daf 67a) s.v. "beginning disqualifications of kehunah"—see there; and their words are closed in their answer there: for what helps that now before us it is nine months—we say in all presumptions even if impaired before us we say "now it was impaired"; and for Taz it is fine presumption of minor stands to be removed in this—one should not establish presumption where it was removed before us. Also in my humble opinion that which Tosafot in Bava Metzia specifically mentioned presumption of pregnant—there birth is the essence of the action and we judge on this whether at time of pulling the cow she was still pregnant or already gave birth; on this we say since moment before pulling the cow she was certainly pregnant, we establish presumption she was also pregnant at pulling; and what we see she gave birth now—now she gave birth; unlike here where doubt whether this offspring is eight days—presumption of pregnant does not decide from this it is not eight days—and this is a fine distinction and correct with God's help; and proof from that Yevamos regarding doubt minor or adult—they did not mention presumption of pregnant to judge it is minor; and also not relevant in our case to say the offspring was in presumption it is not eight days old—we say also now it is not eight days old, for it is simple at every moment it becomes greater in years than before; and only from minor to adult is presumption relevant, for in one moment one exits minority to majority and changed—it is relevant to say we establish on presumption of minor; unlike here where day eight does not change the animal's essence or name. And eight days are to reveal it was never stillborn—but not that there be matter of change and renewal upon reaching eight days—simple there is no presumption. And also in presumption of minority Maharit expanded in responsa (part 1 siman 41) it is not presumption since day by day it grows; and he expanded on Tosafot Yevamos intent above—in any case here regarding eight days, which is not matter of minority and majority, certainly irrelevant to say presumption it is not eight days old. <b>And the essence:</b> what to judge in our case that there is presumption "not slaughtered"—nevertheless in my humble opinion since from Torah we follow majority of offspring—even though rabbis forbade because of doubt they made—nevertheless presumption was already removed through majority and it is mere doubt; and like combining minority to presumption. And that gentile mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed—it must be said truly since root of Torah prohibition, mesisach l'fi tumo does not help—not like Maharshal regarding eggs above, and so proved from hagahah Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah (siman 316) who wrote specifically regarding cows that are not accustomed to be milked without offspring, but regarding goats accustomed to be milked without offspring we do not rely on this—and its root from Maharai (siman 130) explained there: specifically regarding cows because it is not their way at all to be milked without offspring—in this they combine leniency of mesisach l'fi tumo where there is no concern he says so to enhance his merchandise; but regarding goats accustomed to be milked without offspring we do not rely on mesisach l'fi tumo, for he is not believed except in woman's testimony alone—see there. And behold truly there is study: this principle that mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed is specifically in Torah law or rabbinic in presumption of prohibition; but rabbinic without presumption he is believed—see Shach (siman 98 s.k. 2). And if here regarding milking goats—from Torah exempt from bechorah, majority animals are not milked unless they give birth, and rabbinically they were stringent to say here combine minority to presumption and it becomes half and half; and since only rabbinically half and half and there is no presumption of prohibition—why is mesisach l'fi tumo not believed. And it must be said since bechorah prohibition is Torah—even though concern is only rabbinic, nevertheless mesisach l'fi tumo does not help; and Maharai goes according to his view who ruled in Terumat HaDeshen (siman 79) one who buys eggs from gentile on Yom Tov after Shabbat—gentile mesisach l'fi tumo does not help, since preparation is Torah law mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed—see there—even though from Torah there is majority they were already born, only rabbinically forbidden as davar sheyesh lo matirin—nevertheless mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed since its root is Torah; and since Rama ruled like Terumat HaDeshen regarding milking goats, it is proved we rule that in its root Torah and rabbinic concern, mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed—not like Maharshal; and the wonder on Magen Avraham who follows Maharshal's approach is fulfilled—and it implies from Magen Avraham's words what Rama ruled there that gentile mesisach l'fi tumo is not believed on eggs—he holds like Mordechai that there is no majority to permit, for eggs from moment born they eat them; and from this Magen Avraham derived in our countries where many eggs are common, certainly majority brought from those born—heavy mesisach l'fi tumo is believed (and Magen Avraham there hints see Yoreh De'ah siman 15—it appears his intent is our difficulty: if we say in root Torah and rabbinic concern mesisach l'fi tumo is believed, why not believed mesisach l'fi tumo they are eight days). And this is astonishing—for if so, why regarding milking goats is mesisach l'fi tumo not believed—we return to what is not called presumption of prohibition here. And one witness is believed even if called presumption of prohibition—this matter itself that one witness is not believed in place of presumption is an unresolved question not resolved in Yevamos—and here it is rabbinic doubt; and even though Taz (siman 15) called it doubt of one-day-olds that it is davar sheyesh lo matirin—Shach wondered correctly that the permitter is not certainly coming; and apart from this, here from necessity if permission comes he will wait eight days—automatically also now permitted; and even according to Taz that it is davar sheyesh lo matirin—nevertheless specifically in doubt in action we go stringently, but not in legal doubt as Peri Chadash wrote (siman 498 s.k. 3) and responsa She'eilat Ya'avetz (siman 90); and I also brought proof from Ran Nedarim (daf 46b) who wrote regarding one who vows these fruits on his fellow and similarly vows on himself fruits without "these," and he exchanged them—he is permitted, for it is rabbinic doubt; and apparently though regarding vow on fellow it is not davar sheyesh lo matirin, for the forbidden one cannot permit him—but regarding vow on himself behold it is davar sheyesh lo matirin—nevertheless in legal doubt we go leniently; and if so here that is legal doubt, if one witness is believed in presumption of prohibition we go leniently—from all these reasons it appears one witness is believed they are eight days old. And see responsa Rosh (kelal 52 siman 5) who wrote regarding two and two contradicting on woman's offspring whether it lived thirty days—for we follow majority of offspring, behold he was not concerned for presumption of prohibition assisting other witnesses; also according to Rambam (chapter 3 laws of Yibbum) one witness is believed to testify months completed; and so Mechaber ruled (siman 256 seif 5)—and it implies the same regarding testifying it lived thirty days, and all one matter. If so it appears this is exactly our case; and even though Rosh in responsa holds one witness, and as hagahah Shulchan Aruch there wrote—one may say it is different there because matters of forbidden relations are not less than two; and even among poskim who permit there is not such proof for our case—one may say there presumption assists and leans to support. Nevertheless one may say since sages were stringent, one may say regarding animal only because of decree "lest person," as Tosafot Yevamos wrote—and since regarding person itself one witness is believed, so too regarding animal. And what depends on contradictory witnesses—if they came two by two it is contradictory testimony as if they do not exist; and if one permissive witness came first, he is like two and one's words are not in place of two—as stated in Shulchan Aruch below (siman 39) regarding lung adhesions—even though one may say there majority kosher animals assists him, so too majority viable offspring assists; and even according to Ritva brought in Shach (siman 127) who holds in prohibitions one witness is believed and need not live like two—one should not establish like two; nevertheless in Shulchan Aruch (siman 39 there) ruled simply that also in prohibitions it is like two. And in any case from rabbinic doubt nothing emerges whether it is like two; and automatically in rabbinic matters we go leniently; and from davar sheyesh lo matirin side there is no concern as written—as explained in my humble opinion with God's help.`,
});

// --- nekudot-hakesef (3) ---
patchFile('siman_015/nekudot-hakesef/part-001.txt', 'nekudot-hakesef', {
  '1#_': `<b>(Siman 66 s.k. 1) And one may wonder on the Rosh, etc.</b> Requires study—for in chapter Elu Treifot he did not copy except words of Rav Alfas, as known to one who knows our rabbi's way who copies Rif's language anonymously in several places even though it does not seem right to him; and in first chapter of Beitzah it is his essential ruling. And therefore Tur and Beit Yosef and other later authorities wrote in name of Rosh that he ruled like R' Eliezer ben Yaakov—not as he wrote in his name that he rules like first Tanna. And so it appears explicitly from words of Maadanei Melech 4:179b as I wrote; also what he wrote hagahah Ashiri ruled like R' Eliezer ben Yaakov—does not exist, for if so what does he say "and such as born with wings," etc.—it should have said explanation "born with wings," etc.; and further what does he say "Rabbi Yoel forbade because of sheketz"—what praise of Rabbi Yoel who forbade thus, for this is like R' Eliezer ben Yaakov. Rather certainly he says thus: that which R' Eliezer ben Yaakov and rabbis dispute is such as born with wings, etc.; all birds whose wings have not grown, Rabbi Yoel forbade because of sheketz—meaning that although from "creeping thing upon the earth" there is nothing, nevertheless from sheketz and disgust it is forbidden to eat; and so appears from all later authorities who although ruled like first Tanna, copied Hagahot Maor words for halachic ruling.`,
  '2#_': `<b>And he was not precise that Talmud is arranged in chapter 4 of Beitzah daf 34, etc.</b> As if Rama did not see this Talmud—truly Rama saw it, and behold it is explained in Tur and Shulchan Aruch Orach Chaim there seif 8; and Rama in Darkei Moshe brings it and answers it: "so Rambam ruled chapter 2; and Maggid Mishneh wrote so is Rashba's view, and Beit Yosef; and so Rosh; and so we rule, not like Rabbeinu Nissim who ruled forbidden; and so all in it. And it appears to me specifically regarding crushed bird the Rosh permits because we establish it on its original presumption of fitness, and therefore we are not concerned lest it be treifah; but where there is no presumption of fitness—it is forbidden—as I wrote above regarding calf born on Yom Tov forbidden according to Rosh; so it appears to me"—end of Darkei Moshe. And with this what he challenged—what did R' Zeira resolve from checking roof tiles, etc.—certainly the explainers did not seem to him to distinguish thus; however we who doubt the halachah whether leniently or stringently, since it was not resolved in investigation—therefore where there is good presumption we rule leniently, and therefore Rosh ruled there leniently; but regarding calf born where there is no good presumption, he ruled stringently. However in any case requires study—for that which R' Zeira resolved from roof tiles: stam roof tiles too, majority are good and do not crack, and it is like presumption of crushed bird we establish on its presumption, for majority animals are in presumption of fitness—and so majority roof tiles do not crack; unlike calf born that immediately when born it has deficiency and is not in category of majority—and requires study. Also what he challenged Maharshal and expanded in words of lips—the Bach already preceded in all his words in Orach Chaim there, and so Gaon Av Beit Din z"l wrote in responsa.`,
  '3#_': `<b>(s.k. 4) And one may say he holds like one who says, etc.</b> This is not so—for here the permitter is not certainly coming, and in such case it is not davar sheyesh lo matirin at all, as below siman 102 seif 2. But in truth there is no beginning to this question: how can you say "if you find to say not completed, perhaps it has eight—what of it that it has eight, nevertheless not completed and it is stillborn"; and further when you say not completed, it cannot have eight—for when it is eight days old it is removed from doubt of stillborn, as stated in Talmud and poskim in chapter R' Eliezer of Milah and in good places; and perhaps this is included in what he wrote afterward "and one may say a double doubt is not relevant here," etc.; but in truth he did not need to raise it in writing, for it is simpler to express in the bosom of the cloak.`,
});

// --- kaf-hachayim (3) ---
patchFile('siman_015/kaf-hachayim/part-001.txt', 'kaf-hachayim', {
  '1#_': `<b>(1) [Seif 1] A chick—all the time it has not gone out, etc.—forbidden.</b> Because of "the creeping thing that creeps upon the earth." Chullin 64a. Tur. And it implies there that this prohibition is only rabbinic and the verse is mere asmakhta. And so Bach—except he brought from there Seder Torah siman 33 that it is forbidden from Torah because of neveilah, only prohibition because of sheretz is asmakhta—see there; and see in Perishah ot 1 what he expanded on this matter—see there. Therefore for practical law certainly the doubt does not emerge and it is doubt of Torah prohibition; and practical difference if there are two pots, one permitted and one forbidden, and such a chick fell and we do not know into which—it is double doubt: perhaps it did not fall here, and perhaps from Torah it is permitted and its prohibition is rabbinic; and everything rabbinic I say it fell into prohibition and it is permitted. Shulchan Aruch HaRav ot 1; Zivchei Tzedek ot 1; and it appears there must also be majority in the dish—for from Torah it is nullified in majority.`,
  '2#_': `<b>(2) There. A chick—all the time it has not gone out, etc.</b> See below siman 66 seif 2 and siman 86 seif 8; and in our words there b'siyata diShmaya.`,
  '3#_': `<b>(3) There. And after it goes out into the air of the world—it is permitted immediately.</b> It implies even if feathers did not grow and eyes did not open—it is permitted. However in Tur he brought R' Eliezer ben Yaakov's view that we require eyes opened; and wrote so ruled his father the Rosh z"l; but in Beit Yosef he brought several poskim who hold like first Tanna that we do not require eyes opened; and wrote so we rule, and therefore ruled so in Shulchan Aruch. However he wrote in Hagahot Maor first chapter of Beitzah: "and such as born with wings—that is, large feather on its body that has quills; for all birds whose wings have not grown, Rabbi Yoel forbade because of sheketz"—end of his words, Or Zarua. And Beit Din, Bach wrote so we rule. And so Shach s.k. 2; Peri Chadash ot 2; Perishah ot 1; Lev Pri ot 1; Binyan Yehoshua ot 1; She'eirit Menachem ot 1; place of Shmuel in Chiddushei Dinim for Yoreh De'ah this siman. Mishnat Hashem ot 1; Machberet Avraham ot 1; Erech HaShulchan ot 2; Kometz HaMinchah ot 1; Zivchei Shabbat ot 1; and if slaughtered b'dieved before feathers grew—Perishah there permits; but She'eirit Menachem there wrote they are forbidden; and so Kometz HaMinchah. Zivchei Tzedek ot 2.`,
});

// --- yad-avraham (2) ---
patchFile('siman_015/yad-avraham/part-001.txt', 'yad-avraham', {
  '1#_': `(Siman 15 seif 2) <b>And if it is not known that its months are completed—it is forbidden because of doubt of stillborn until beginning of the eighth night.</b> In Birkhei Yosef he wrote: a kid slaughtered and two witnesses disputed whether it was eight days old—permitted through double doubt. And one may say for our case that Rashba regarding gentile mesisach l'fi tumo—and it appears this is because Taz wrote there it is davar sheyesh lo matirin, and this is when not yet slaughtered; unlike when slaughtered it is not davar sheyesh lo matirin and double doubt helps. And if so, similarly in doubt whether it was nine months one may permit from Torah. And in book Tzitz Eliezer he permits for another reason. But according to Pri Megadim in Sha'ar HaTefillah that there is no double doubt where sages concerned for minority—if so there is no double doubt even after slaughter.`,
  '2#_': `(Seif 3) <b>We do not rely on a gentile regarding small kids purchased from him who says they are eight days old.</b> And even mesisach l'fi tumo he is not believed in Torah prohibition—Shach; and Pri Megadim wrote it appears from his words that all who say it is not known whether months completed is a mistaken presumption that if rabbinic, gentile mesisach l'fi tumo is believed as written in siman 98 and 137. But it is impossible to say so as proved in Shulchan Aruch siman 256 and 366, and so in book Beit Avraham. And they did not see Knesset HaGedolah already sensed this—for it is proved there majority of offspring are not stillborn, only rabbinically we concern for minority; and where there is majority we rely on gentile mesisach l'fi tumo as stated in Orach Chaim siman 513 regarding gentile selling eggs on Yom Tov 1. And so in responsa Tiferet Tzvi; and Pri Megadim wrote possibly this presumption is called that until now it was pregnant—and so in book Tiferet Tzvi. But this is certainly not so—for then in Orach Chaim there regarding eggs sold on Yom Tov we would also say thus; and explicitly Ran wrote an egg does not become presumption of prohibition—from which presumption of pregnant does not help; and now she gave birth—and this is because we say establish the offspring in presumption of permission it had in its mother's womb. And similarly Tosafot beginning of Bechoros wrote. But one may say according to what Noda B'Yehuda wrote part Even HaEzer siman 19 regarding stillborn of animal there is presumption of prohibition because it was in presumption it is not slaughtered, and we combine minority of stillborns to presumption it is not slaughtered—for if stillborn shechitah does not help; in present minority all say combine minority to presumption. And therefore one may say we do not rely here on gentile mesisach l'fi tumo—for there is presumption of prohibition. And this is not as Tiferet Moshe wrote simply that here there is no presumption of prohibition—for also on day one it is permitted if known months completed, and while alive until day eight the matter is revealed retroactively it was never stillborn and was before eight like a piece of doubt milk—end of his words. And according to Noda B'Yehuda's view as above.`,
});

// --- yad-ephraim (2) ---
patchFile('siman_015/yad-ephraim/part-001.txt', 'yad-ephraim', {
  '1#_': `<b>Until beginning of the eighth night.</b> Bach; and Noda B'Yehuda wrote part Yoreh De'ah siman 7 regarding calf born day 1 an hour before night—it is proved in several places that we taught seven days do not require from time to time, as Taz's view on this; and if so permitted to slaughter at Motzei Shabbat; however l'chatchila one should not act, for Pri Megadim's view requires from time to time specifically; and one may be stringent l'chatchila; and nevertheless one who rules leniently does not lose; and also since those seven days are rabbinic—for from Torah majority of offspring are not stillborn—see there; and so in last Beit Shmuel siman 11 wrote and agreed from time to time is not required; and explained from his words that such is the custom; and see in book Beit Avraham on treifot in responsa compendium that I expanded on this matter and brought Rambam's words that appear from his words it is Torah law except it is doubt stillborn and we do not flog on doubt; but Torah doubt nevertheless exists; and I expanded on Talmud and poskim words there regarding whether to rely on mesisach l'fi tumo that they are eight days—see there; and see in responsa Bigdei Kehunah what he wrote to reconcile Shach's words that not completed months, eight days do not help—this is against Tosafot Shabbat—see there. <b>He wrote</b> in She'eilat Ya'avetz part 2 siman 56 regarding calves that are not eight days old that they are accustomed to permit slaughtering within eight days to take their stomach to set cheeses, and the seller does not wish to wait until the calf is eight days old but sells immediately when born; and if Jews do not buy, gentiles will buy—and he wrote if possible to appease him to wait, one should do so; and if he does not wish, then slaughter with certain kashrut; and if not, do not bless on the shechitah lest they come to permit the meat for eating—only puncture them; and see siman 119—see there. And he wrote in Tiferet Tzvi siman 67 regarding calves where there is doubt whether they are eight days and whether to rely on permission of horns and teeth, etc.; and there siman 68 he also wrote on this—see there; and see in book responsa Beit Avraham siman 3 that I wrote when it became clear that in those places signs are not clear and it is common to find horns and teeth even before eight days—one who is lenient on this will give judgment in the future—see there. And regarding relying on estimation—for need one may be lenient from mouth of expert slaughterer expert in nature of calves' growth in the place where he resides, and it is clear to him it cannot be less than seven days old—provided he is presumed in fear of Heaven not to treat estimation lightly—see there.`,
  '2#_': `<b>And says they are eight days old.</b> Bach; and see in book responsa Beit Avraham in responsa compendium siman 3, and there I brought Shach's words in Nekudat HaKesef that there is no double doubt: "if you find to say not completed, perhaps it has eight days—what of it that it has eight days, nevertheless not completed and it is stillborn," etc.; and in Sha'ar HaMelech laws of Yom Tov he challenged this from Tosafot Shabbat s.v. "from when," etc.—see there in my books; and see in responsa Bigdei Kehunah part Yoreh De'ah siman 1 on this matter.`,
});

